WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.080
John G Maguire comes from a family of news reporters and writers.

2
00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:10.255
His father, the late John Maguire,

3
00:00:10.255 --> 00:00:13.440
wrote for the Albany New York Times Union.

4
00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:17.310
His brother Gregory Maguire is a novelist whose work

5
00:00:17.310 --> 00:00:21.290
includes the book Wicked from which a hit musical has been made.

6
00:00:21.290 --> 00:00:24.810
John Maguire has been a professional writer all his life.

7
00:00:24.810 --> 00:00:27.570
He began in college as a physics major,

8
00:00:27.570 --> 00:00:31.480
but switched before graduation to study in English literature.

9
00:00:31.480 --> 00:00:35.005
He then pursued a career as a newspaper science writer.

10
00:00:35.005 --> 00:00:39.505
He's covered science news for three daily newspapers in the US,

11
00:00:39.505 --> 00:00:42.870
was nominated for a Pulitzer Prize in journalism,

12
00:00:42.870 --> 00:00:45.020
and he's written several books.

13
00:00:45.020 --> 00:00:49.700
He's also taught writing for many years at Boston area colleges.

14
00:00:49.700 --> 00:00:52.820
When John was teaching at the Berkley College of Music,

15
00:00:52.820 --> 00:00:58.675
he invented a new approach to help sometimes confused and worried students to write well,

16
00:00:58.675 --> 00:01:00.955
it's called the Readability Method.

17
00:01:00.955 --> 00:01:05.404
It was published by the Education Department of Newsweek Magazine.

18
00:01:05.404 --> 00:01:10.790
His latest version of this approach is described in his College Writing Guide.

19
00:01:10.790 --> 00:01:14.795
John has a Bachelors and a Master of Fine Arts degree in English.

20
00:01:14.795 --> 00:01:17.990
He currently has an editing business and teaches writing at

21
00:01:17.990 --> 00:01:22.100
Middlesex Community College in Bedford Massachusetts.

22
00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:24.280
Let's welcome John Maguire.

23
00:01:24.280 --> 00:01:28.619
John, you wrote a wonderful article for the Atlantic,

24
00:01:28.619 --> 00:01:30.735
The secret to good writing.

25
00:01:30.735 --> 00:01:33.515
It's about objects, not ideas.

26
00:01:33.515 --> 00:01:36.260
This is pretty counter-intuitive for a lot of people.

27
00:01:36.260 --> 00:01:38.990
I mean, most of us have been brought up to think that

28
00:01:38.990 --> 00:01:42.745
writing is all about the good ideas.

29
00:01:42.745 --> 00:01:44.785
So, can you explain what you meant?

30
00:01:44.785 --> 00:01:49.380
Yes Barbara, it does seem strange for a writing teacher to say,

31
00:01:49.380 --> 00:01:53.215
think about objects more than you're thinking about ideas.

32
00:01:53.215 --> 00:01:55.870
Of course, it is important to write about ideas.

33
00:01:55.870 --> 00:01:58.950
But my point in that article was to say,

34
00:01:59.740 --> 00:02:04.550
it's easy to express ideas in terms of objects and what a good writer

35
00:02:04.550 --> 00:02:09.290
thinks about is what objects can I use to express my ideas.

36
00:02:09.290 --> 00:02:12.995
It's not bad to use idea words,

37
00:02:12.995 --> 00:02:17.670
but it's easy to get overrun with idea words and use too many of them.

38
00:02:17.670 --> 00:02:20.715
You can become over-abstract,

39
00:02:20.715 --> 00:02:23.210
and when you're writing becomes over-abstract and there were

40
00:02:23.210 --> 00:02:28.590
too many -ion words or -tion words in a given passage,

41
00:02:28.590 --> 00:02:31.700
it becomes very hard for the reader to know what's going on,

42
00:02:31.700 --> 00:02:36.370
and in fact the writer often gets confused about what he or she is saying.

43
00:02:36.370 --> 00:02:38.625
When things become very abstract,

44
00:02:38.625 --> 00:02:41.860
they become very blurry and both the reader and the writer has

45
00:02:41.860 --> 00:02:45.815
to spend a lot of time figuring out what the heck is being said.

46
00:02:45.815 --> 00:02:50.320
The point I wanted to make is that all ideas are related to objects.

47
00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:52.640
Let me show you a diagram.

48
00:02:55.530 --> 00:03:06.865
Here on a white board is what we call the ladder of abstractions.

49
00:03:06.865 --> 00:03:09.880
This is a ladder that ruins from low to high,

50
00:03:09.880 --> 00:03:14.980
and at the top end of the ladder are abstract ideas,

51
00:03:14.980 --> 00:03:17.079
let's say the idea of nutrition.

52
00:03:17.079 --> 00:03:22.485
Now, an abstract idea is a non-physical thing that you cannot touch.

53
00:03:22.485 --> 00:03:26.745
At the bottom of the ladder are connected to the word nutrition,

54
00:03:26.745 --> 00:03:29.190
actual things like apples,

55
00:03:29.190 --> 00:03:34.090
oranges, slices of bread, hamburger sandwiches.

56
00:03:34.090 --> 00:03:36.500
Any idea that you're trying to get across,

57
00:03:36.500 --> 00:03:40.755
let us say you are writing about good nutrition for pregnant women,

58
00:03:40.755 --> 00:03:45.500
can be communicated in terms of things.

59
00:03:45.500 --> 00:03:49.885
The principle that writers follow is when you're writing about ideas,

60
00:03:49.885 --> 00:03:52.880
go down the ladder to the world of things,

61
00:03:52.880 --> 00:03:55.355
and make sure you write with enough things.

62
00:03:55.355 --> 00:03:58.294
So, in our course,

63
00:03:58.294 --> 00:04:02.950
we suggest to learners that they should alternate between sort of close in

64
00:04:02.950 --> 00:04:08.425
focus in a more diffuse approach to learning in order to learn something difficult.

65
00:04:08.425 --> 00:04:16.645
So, toggling between a tight attention and a loose attention seems to really work,

66
00:04:16.645 --> 00:04:21.385
you've told me that professional writers seem to do the same sort of switching.

67
00:04:21.385 --> 00:04:26.440
Can you tell us how a writer switches between focused and relaxed thinking?

68
00:04:26.440 --> 00:04:31.780
Any creative person switches from one mode to another frequently.

69
00:04:31.780 --> 00:04:34.610
Maybe the best example of this is painters.

70
00:04:34.610 --> 00:04:39.670
The classic cliche image of a painter is somebody who's up close to

71
00:04:39.670 --> 00:04:45.530
the canvas putting a little dot of paint on and then he backs up halfway across the room,

72
00:04:45.530 --> 00:04:49.430
takes a look at what the effect has been of that dot of paint,

73
00:04:49.430 --> 00:04:51.020
thinks about it, weighs it,

74
00:04:51.020 --> 00:04:54.320
goes back in and put some more dots of paint on the painting.

75
00:04:54.320 --> 00:05:00.110
The shifting of focus from up-close too far back is obvious in the world of a painter.

76
00:05:00.110 --> 00:05:03.530
You have spoken about how important that is in the world

77
00:05:03.530 --> 00:05:06.965
of math and how when you are learning equations,

78
00:05:06.965 --> 00:05:10.745
it's a good idea to come in close and really master.

79
00:05:10.745 --> 00:05:14.120
What you need to know when sometimes it's a good idea to pull back in

80
00:05:14.120 --> 00:05:17.780
order to understand the problems that you have to solve with math.

81
00:05:17.780 --> 00:05:22.290
Writers switch focus many different ways.

82
00:05:22.410 --> 00:05:26.200
Sometimes you will turn from what you're trying to say and you'll

83
00:05:26.200 --> 00:05:29.625
talk to your friends and ask your friends what they know about the subject,

84
00:05:29.625 --> 00:05:33.575
that broadens out the discussion of what you're trying to say.

85
00:05:33.575 --> 00:05:37.435
Sometimes you will shift and do a little bit of reading.

86
00:05:37.435 --> 00:05:40.550
Maybe go on Wikipedia for five minutes to

87
00:05:40.550 --> 00:05:43.620
look up some material that relates to what you're talking about.

88
00:05:43.620 --> 00:05:48.245
Sometimes you will shift from writing on a computer to writing longhand.

89
00:05:48.245 --> 00:05:53.150
I sometimes shift from writing while sitting down to writing while standing up,

90
00:05:53.150 --> 00:05:55.100
and of course there's the tried and true,

91
00:05:55.100 --> 00:05:57.310
let's take a walk around the block.

92
00:05:57.310 --> 00:05:59.800
Charles Dickens did it and if he did it,

93
00:05:59.800 --> 00:06:03.845
it's good enough for me and for college writers, I would say.

94
00:06:03.845 --> 00:06:08.900
I see. So, beyond concrete nouns or things,

95
00:06:08.900 --> 00:06:10.310
what other advice do you give your

96
00:06:10.310 --> 00:06:13.735
students about what they should put into their writing?

97
00:06:13.735 --> 00:06:17.345
Barbara, writing well is a matter of choosing a good style,

98
00:06:17.345 --> 00:06:21.480
and style is basically the choice of what kind of words you're going to write with,

99
00:06:21.480 --> 00:06:24.685
what parameters are you going to have control over.

100
00:06:24.685 --> 00:06:27.349
So, beyond putting in things,

101
00:06:27.349 --> 00:06:29.190
which is very important,

102
00:06:29.190 --> 00:06:30.570
I tell my students,

103
00:06:30.570 --> 00:06:32.305
put in active verbs,

104
00:06:32.305 --> 00:06:35.680
these are verbs that makes something happen on the page.

105
00:06:35.680 --> 00:06:38.240
I also tell them to put in people.

106
00:06:38.240 --> 00:06:42.280
How do you put people into a piece of writing? You name them.

107
00:06:42.280 --> 00:06:46.365
Full name or nickname,

108
00:06:46.365 --> 00:06:48.925
names of people, put them into your writing.

109
00:06:48.925 --> 00:06:50.290
Use some active verbs,

110
00:06:50.290 --> 00:06:52.390
make things happen on the page.

111
00:06:52.390 --> 00:06:55.290
Other pieces of advice I suggest are,

112
00:06:55.290 --> 00:06:58.600
use short words when you can instead of long ones,

113
00:06:58.600 --> 00:07:00.695
and use short sentences.

114
00:07:00.695 --> 00:07:03.430
It's often important especially for beginning

115
00:07:03.430 --> 00:07:07.870
writers not to let their sentences get out of control too long.

116
00:07:07.870 --> 00:07:10.845
When in doubt, write it short.

117
00:07:10.845 --> 00:07:16.795
Have you ever taught students for whom English was their second language?

118
00:07:16.795 --> 00:07:21.900
What can you tell a student has learned to write outside an English-speaking country?

119
00:07:21.900 --> 00:07:27.865
Yes, I have taught people to write who were ESL speakers.

120
00:07:27.865 --> 00:07:33.310
One of the more interesting friends I made recently was a young man named Saktai,

121
00:07:33.310 --> 00:07:36.430
I taught him last semester in the city of Lowell.

122
00:07:36.430 --> 00:07:40.060
The first papers he turned in were really pretty terrible.

123
00:07:40.060 --> 00:07:43.420
I asked him why he wrote that way and he told me

124
00:07:43.420 --> 00:07:46.835
he had been taught to write that way by his teacher back in,

125
00:07:46.835 --> 00:07:48.725
I think it was Vietnam.

126
00:07:48.725 --> 00:07:52.440
I said, you are out of control with what you're saying here,

127
00:07:52.440 --> 00:07:54.255
you have to be simpler.

128
00:07:54.255 --> 00:07:57.650
He told me that his teacher had told him

129
00:07:57.650 --> 00:08:01.855
make sure you use your long words and show people that you're educated,

130
00:08:01.855 --> 00:08:03.930
that's the way to write.

131
00:08:03.930 --> 00:08:06.275
I said to him, Saktai,

132
00:08:06.275 --> 00:08:08.845
that's not the way we do it in English.

133
00:08:08.845 --> 00:08:13.825
In English, we really favor the plain style which means clear and simple.

134
00:08:13.825 --> 00:08:16.145
So, I'm going to give you some advice.

135
00:08:16.145 --> 00:08:18.835
Young man, I said,

136
00:08:18.835 --> 00:08:20.675
trying to get his attention,

137
00:08:20.675 --> 00:08:26.425
I want you to stop trying to be impressive and just start trying to be clear.

138
00:08:26.425 --> 00:08:31.280
I had to say it a couple of times and I told him this,

139
00:08:31.280 --> 00:08:33.020
I said, once you are clear,

140
00:08:33.020 --> 00:08:34.635
then you're going to be impressive.

141
00:08:34.635 --> 00:08:36.370
So, stop trying to be impressive,

142
00:08:36.370 --> 00:08:39.400
just work on being completely clear.

143
00:08:39.420 --> 00:08:41.490
About four weeks later,

144
00:08:41.490 --> 00:08:46.955
his writing had improved quite a bit and he came to me and said,

145
00:08:46.955 --> 00:08:50.270
that the advice that I've given him to stop trying to be

146
00:08:50.270 --> 00:08:52.880
impressive and just to be clear had really worked.

147
00:08:52.880 --> 00:08:55.150
Nobody had ever told him that before,

148
00:08:55.150 --> 00:08:56.920
he really needed to hear it.

149
00:08:56.920 --> 00:09:02.780
Now, he understood what he had to do and he felt much more powerful as a writer.

150
00:09:02.780 --> 00:09:05.420
Instead of trying to do two things at once,

151
00:09:05.420 --> 00:09:07.340
which was to be clear and to be impressive,

152
00:09:07.340 --> 00:09:09.260
he just tried to be clear.

153
00:09:09.260 --> 00:09:13.020
As we both know, when you read something that is really clear,

154
00:09:13.020 --> 00:09:14.380
after you finish reading it,

155
00:09:14.380 --> 00:09:18.155
you say, wow, that was impressive,

156
00:09:18.155 --> 00:09:21.720
and that's the way it works, clarity first.

157
00:09:21.720 --> 00:09:24.015
What a great point.

158
00:09:24.015 --> 00:09:28.240
It is funny when I read research papers,

159
00:09:28.240 --> 00:09:32.470
I can always tell a difference between the second tier,

160
00:09:32.470 --> 00:09:35.780
the lower caliber, research papers and

161
00:09:35.780 --> 00:09:42.460
the upper tier research papers because the lower caliber ones are often fuzzy,

162
00:09:42.460 --> 00:09:44.090
they use a lot of big words,

163
00:09:44.090 --> 00:09:46.230
they're hard to read, they're hard to understand.

164
00:09:46.230 --> 00:09:50.010
But, the ones that really are top notch,

165
00:09:50.010 --> 00:09:53.910
they're often so simple and so elegantly written,

166
00:09:53.910 --> 00:09:56.170
and you might say,

167
00:09:56.170 --> 00:10:00.210
well, that's not academic writing because it's too elegant.

168
00:10:00.210 --> 00:10:05.159
But, that's actually the very best of writing in any discipline.

169
00:10:05.159 --> 00:10:08.580
So, let me ask you about this,

170
00:10:08.580 --> 00:10:14.050
some teachers tell students not to edit as they write because they think it's a bad idea.

171
00:10:14.050 --> 00:10:15.880
How do you feel about that?

172
00:10:15.880 --> 00:10:18.720
I agree with those coaches who say,

173
00:10:18.720 --> 00:10:21.755
separate the writing from the editing.

174
00:10:21.755 --> 00:10:27.250
Writing is a matter of getting things down on the paper or on the screen rapidly.

175
00:10:27.250 --> 00:10:30.575
You want to maintain rapid forward progress.

176
00:10:30.575 --> 00:10:33.950
You don't want to get stuck and lose your momentum.

177
00:10:33.950 --> 00:10:37.185
Editing is a process of being judgmental,

178
00:10:37.185 --> 00:10:39.225
deciding whether things are working.

179
00:10:39.225 --> 00:10:42.425
If you're not a very good writer if you're a beginning writer,

180
00:10:42.425 --> 00:10:47.305
it's possible to produce a bad sentence and then be totally stymied about how to fix it,

181
00:10:47.305 --> 00:10:52.635
and spend minutes puzzling over something stupid that you've written.

182
00:10:52.635 --> 00:10:57.795
Far better to choose to maintain your momentum roll forward,

183
00:10:57.795 --> 00:11:01.195
you want to get the conveyor belt of ideas rolling.

184
00:11:01.195 --> 00:11:06.690
After lots of material has come out of your idea factory and your writing factory,

185
00:11:06.690 --> 00:11:10.575
then you can change gears and edit.

186
00:11:10.575 --> 00:11:14.150
I have another idea about editing which is that it's

187
00:11:14.150 --> 00:11:18.195
often useful to print out a draft and edit on paper.

188
00:11:18.195 --> 00:11:20.650
I do that.

189
00:11:20.650 --> 00:11:24.000
I think a great many professional writers will print out a draft,

190
00:11:24.000 --> 00:11:31.005
edit it on paper and then enter their edits back into the computer version of the text.

191
00:11:31.005 --> 00:11:34.150
That sounds like great advice I know for me when I'm

192
00:11:34.150 --> 00:11:37.219
writing and I find myself really getting stuck,

193
00:11:37.219 --> 00:11:41.485
it's generally because I'm starting to edit while I'm writing,

194
00:11:41.485 --> 00:11:47.255
and so often I'll find that if I just let go and let it come out,

195
00:11:47.255 --> 00:11:52.140
later on mostly I find it's not as bad as I thought when I was writing it.

196
00:11:52.140 --> 00:11:53.935
Because usually when it comes out I'm thinking,

197
00:11:53.935 --> 00:12:00.235
oh it's terrible but it comes out and I do a little tweaking and it's not too bad.

198
00:12:00.235 --> 00:12:04.495
So, suppose a student really wants to write better,

199
00:12:04.495 --> 00:12:10.865
is there some special technique of reading that will help improve your writing?

200
00:12:10.865 --> 00:12:16.790
Well, read closely the people you admire.

201
00:12:16.790 --> 00:12:19.130
If you're writing short stories,

202
00:12:19.130 --> 00:12:21.245
read the short stories you admire.

203
00:12:21.245 --> 00:12:24.319
But if you're writing scientific papers

204
00:12:24.319 --> 00:12:28.295
or term papers things to be published in journals,

205
00:12:28.295 --> 00:12:31.130
read the writers whom you admire.

206
00:12:31.130 --> 00:12:34.280
If there is somebody who is great in your field and he has

207
00:12:34.280 --> 00:12:38.575
produced very interesting papers that read well,

208
00:12:38.575 --> 00:12:41.790
your job is to study what he or she did.

209
00:12:41.790 --> 00:12:47.635
I would recommend looking closely at the openings of papers that you like,

210
00:12:47.635 --> 00:12:52.100
perhaps copying the opening paragraph down longhand.

211
00:12:52.100 --> 00:12:55.920
I would also recommend underlining words that

212
00:12:55.920 --> 00:12:59.575
he uses that you might not use but you can consider using.

213
00:12:59.575 --> 00:13:05.210
I would recommend you study your writer's sentence length.

214
00:13:05.210 --> 00:13:07.030
How long are his sentences?

215
00:13:07.030 --> 00:13:13.470
What is the pattern of long sentences and short sentences that your a hero uses?

216
00:13:13.470 --> 00:13:17.230
I would recommend looking for a sentence that he wrote that you would think

217
00:13:17.230 --> 00:13:20.995
you could never write because it's not in your nature,

218
00:13:20.995 --> 00:13:26.345
and then go and write that sentence out long end a couple of times.

219
00:13:26.345 --> 00:13:29.215
Study what you admire.

220
00:13:29.215 --> 00:13:31.410
So what if you're writing a paper on

221
00:13:31.410 --> 00:13:38.205
a scientific topic which focuses around something like molecules.

222
00:13:38.205 --> 00:13:40.340
They're too small to be dropped on your foot.

223
00:13:40.340 --> 00:13:43.390
How can you make that somehow more concrete?

224
00:13:43.390 --> 00:13:46.725
Good writers are always thinking of their readers.

225
00:13:46.725 --> 00:13:48.260
A good writer is thinking,

226
00:13:48.260 --> 00:13:50.405
how's my reader going to understand this?

227
00:13:50.405 --> 00:13:52.905
A good writer is thinking,

228
00:13:52.905 --> 00:13:57.385
what is my reader going to see in his head when he reads my words?

229
00:13:57.385 --> 00:14:00.490
So this can produce a problem if you're writing on

230
00:14:00.490 --> 00:14:03.790
a scientific topic say about molecules.

231
00:14:03.790 --> 00:14:08.315
After all molecules are too small to be dropped on your foot.

232
00:14:08.315 --> 00:14:11.910
There's no excuse to stop thinking physically.

233
00:14:11.910 --> 00:14:17.460
You can if you're writing about a simple molecule like the molecule of water.

234
00:14:17.460 --> 00:14:20.240
You can make a visual metaphor,

235
00:14:20.240 --> 00:14:26.820
a grapefruit with two lemons attached to it can stand for H_2O,

236
00:14:26.820 --> 00:14:29.050
or you can go looking for

237
00:14:29.050 --> 00:14:33.250
the physical objects that are related to the chemical research you're writing about.

238
00:14:33.250 --> 00:14:38.645
You can put in as appropriate test tubes or

239
00:14:38.645 --> 00:14:46.150
bunsen burners or beakers or spectrophotometers or the laboratory bench.

240
00:14:46.150 --> 00:14:51.720
It's a skill to go looking for physical correlates to your topic.

241
00:14:51.720 --> 00:14:54.880
But it's a skill that you can get good at pretty quick,

242
00:14:54.880 --> 00:14:59.710
and it pays off because you always want to give the reader something to see.

243
00:14:59.710 --> 00:15:02.765
I think that's very helpful advice.

244
00:15:02.765 --> 00:15:04.325
So, tell me now.

245
00:15:04.325 --> 00:15:08.310
What's more important, the words or the ideas?

246
00:15:09.220 --> 00:15:12.694
As far as a writer is concerned,

247
00:15:12.694 --> 00:15:17.055
there are no ideas outside of the realm of words.

248
00:15:17.055 --> 00:15:22.880
Now I will grant you there are wordless intuitions and little images you might get,

249
00:15:22.880 --> 00:15:27.720
there are diagrams you can put on a piece of paper to capture an idea.

250
00:15:27.720 --> 00:15:30.110
But when it comes to being a writer,

251
00:15:30.110 --> 00:15:33.825
your job is to make sure everything gets down in words.

252
00:15:33.825 --> 00:15:38.120
So if you would ask me which is more important in the paper the words or the ideas?

253
00:15:38.120 --> 00:15:39.740
I say it's the words.

254
00:15:39.740 --> 00:15:42.710
I'd say without the right words in the right order,

255
00:15:42.710 --> 00:15:45.670
you do not have any ideas to work with.

256
00:15:45.670 --> 00:15:51.339
As a writer if you don't have it down in a sentence,

257
00:15:51.339 --> 00:15:53.650
you don't have an idea.

258
00:15:53.650 --> 00:15:56.085
Very good advice.

259
00:15:56.085 --> 00:16:01.565
So, some coaches talk about the problem of having too little to say.

260
00:16:01.565 --> 00:16:06.525
What if a student writer discovers that he or she has too little to say,

261
00:16:06.525 --> 00:16:11.430
what or how can she or he deal with that?

262
00:16:11.710 --> 00:16:16.040
I think a student who has "too little to

263
00:16:16.040 --> 00:16:21.785
say" is usually a student whose imagination is too narrow.

264
00:16:21.785 --> 00:16:24.790
Often it's a student who has decided to write about

265
00:16:24.790 --> 00:16:29.715
an abstract topic and has just kept focusing on the abstraction.

266
00:16:29.715 --> 00:16:33.380
When a student seems to be devoid of ideas,

267
00:16:33.380 --> 00:16:35.580
when a writer is devoid of ideas,

268
00:16:35.580 --> 00:16:43.780
he or she should slow down and brainstorm wildly.

269
00:16:44.600 --> 00:16:49.425
Go to a piece of paper and write down everything you know about the topic,

270
00:16:49.425 --> 00:16:51.630
draw diagrams on the piece of paper,

271
00:16:51.630 --> 00:16:55.130
draw idea maps or mind maps,

272
00:16:55.130 --> 00:16:57.850
make lists of objects,

273
00:16:57.850 --> 00:17:00.375
shift the focus in other words.

274
00:17:00.375 --> 00:17:03.425
A student who doesn't have enough to say,

275
00:17:03.425 --> 00:17:07.205
has often gotten too tense in trying to say something important.

276
00:17:07.205 --> 00:17:09.620
It's a good idea to stop trying to say something

277
00:17:09.620 --> 00:17:13.060
important than just try to say something simple and clear.

278
00:17:13.060 --> 00:17:16.660
You can find your way to be simple and clear by talking

279
00:17:16.660 --> 00:17:20.485
with a friend or by taking a walk as I said.

280
00:17:20.485 --> 00:17:24.105
Another thing to do is to write some questions down for yourself.

281
00:17:24.105 --> 00:17:28.540
You don't have to be an expert on what you're writing about. You just have to be clear.

282
00:17:28.540 --> 00:17:30.035
Write down what puzzles you,

283
00:17:30.035 --> 00:17:32.580
write down what confuses you.

284
00:17:32.580 --> 00:17:35.170
I am confused about X,

285
00:17:35.170 --> 00:17:38.315
I am puzzled about how X relates to Y.

286
00:17:38.315 --> 00:17:39.895
Write these things down,

287
00:17:39.895 --> 00:17:46.340
cover several pieces of paper with ideas and sketches.

288
00:17:46.340 --> 00:17:48.860
It can even look like this.

289
00:17:48.860 --> 00:17:54.065
Tape them together, cover a bunch of paper with notes,

290
00:17:54.065 --> 00:17:59.885
tape them together, set them down on your desk and go out for a half an hours walk.

291
00:17:59.885 --> 00:18:02.090
When you come back, start again.

292
00:18:02.090 --> 00:18:04.360
You will have more ideas.

293
00:18:04.360 --> 00:18:07.005
I like that approach.

294
00:18:07.005 --> 00:18:12.230
So do you have any final takeaways or advice for writing students.

295
00:18:12.230 --> 00:18:16.305
What's one thing a student writer can do to write better?

296
00:18:16.305 --> 00:18:21.045
I'll give you a couple of things a writer can do to make things better.

297
00:18:21.045 --> 00:18:22.220
In terms of readability,

298
00:18:22.220 --> 00:18:25.605
I would suggest using short sentences.

299
00:18:25.605 --> 00:18:30.665
A writer ought to go into this paper and if he has written any really long sentences,

300
00:18:30.665 --> 00:18:33.020
ask himself if these sentences have to be this

301
00:18:33.020 --> 00:18:36.415
long and replace them with shorter sentences.

302
00:18:36.415 --> 00:18:39.490
In terms of completeness,

303
00:18:39.490 --> 00:18:42.270
a writer can do two things.

304
00:18:42.270 --> 00:18:44.795
It's asking himself a couple of questions.

305
00:18:44.795 --> 00:18:49.430
The first one is, what am I trying to say?

306
00:18:49.430 --> 00:18:51.410
Did I say it clearly?

307
00:18:51.410 --> 00:18:53.270
The second one is,

308
00:18:53.270 --> 00:18:56.090
what must I not leave out?

309
00:18:56.090 --> 00:18:58.740
The very last question which is,

310
00:18:58.740 --> 00:19:02.955
what must I make sure not to leave out? It's very useful.

311
00:19:02.955 --> 00:19:09.045
I use it myself all the time as I get to the end of the draft and then ask myself,

312
00:19:09.045 --> 00:19:11.725
did I cover everything in particular?

313
00:19:11.725 --> 00:19:14.965
Is there anything I must make sure not to leave out?

314
00:19:14.965 --> 00:19:18.250
Often it turns out I have left out something important.

315
00:19:18.250 --> 00:19:21.190
So, those are my final words on this.

316
00:19:21.190 --> 00:19:24.280
I think that's a helpful approach even for me.

317
00:19:24.280 --> 00:19:28.110
So I just want to emphasize how

318
00:19:28.110 --> 00:19:33.405
important I think good writing actually is to the learning process.

319
00:19:33.405 --> 00:19:37.590
I often find myself that I really learn

320
00:19:37.590 --> 00:19:41.960
something in the best way when I attempt to write about it.

321
00:19:41.960 --> 00:19:46.980
So, I think this is why insight from people like you who've

322
00:19:46.980 --> 00:19:53.130
really worked to understand the writing process here is your book,

323
00:19:53.130 --> 00:19:56.700
College Writing Guide, it's very very helpful.

324
00:19:56.700 --> 00:19:59.210
So here you can see clearly now,

325
00:19:59.210 --> 00:20:04.950
and so I just very much appreciate John

326
00:20:04.950 --> 00:20:11.880
your interview with me here today and thank you we'll see you on the flip side.